# Postgres connection (docker-compose default below).
#
# IMPORTANT for multi-business deployments (Phase 12): tenant isolation is
# enforced by Postgres row-level security, which superusers and BYPASSRLS
# roles ignore entirely — every policy becomes a silent no-op. The `pos` role
# that docker-compose creates IS a superuser, so it is fine for local
# single-business development but must NOT be what a hosted deployment
# connects as. Provision an unprivileged role and point this at it:
#
#   APP_DB_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 32) npm run db:app-role
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://pos_app:<that password>@localhost:5432/pos
#
# Migrations keep running as the owner (they create tables; the app must not).
# The server refuses to start in production if this role can bypass RLS.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://pos:pos@localhost:5432/pos

# Password for the unprivileged application role created by `npm run db:app-role`.
# Only needed when running that script.
# APP_DB_PASSWORD=

# Role name for the same script. Defaults to pos_app.
# APP_DB_USER=pos_app

# Secret used to sign JWT session cookies. MUST be changed in production.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_SECRET=change-me-in-production

# Owner/Manager session lifetime (hours). Default 12.
SESSION_HOURS=12

# Biometric login / WebAuthn (Phase 20 Wave 3). One deployment is one domain
# (see JWT_SECRET above — the whole app already shares one signing key across
# every business), so one RP ID/origin pair covers every business too.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID: the domain staff sign in on (no scheme/port) — defaults to
#   ROOT_DOMAIN when that is set (the value per-business origins require), and
#   to "localhost" otherwise, for local development.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME: shown by the OS/browser's own biometric prompt.
# WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN: the exact origin(s) (scheme+host+port) requests will come
#   from, comma-separated if there is more than one (e.g. a staging + prod
#   domain) — defaults to "http://localhost:3000". Must match what the
#   browser's address bar shows exactly, or every ceremony fails verification.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost
# WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=سیستم فروش کافه و رستوران
# WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Self-service business registration (Phase 12). Off unless set to exactly
# "true": a deployment being *capable* of many businesses doesn't mean anyone
# may create one. Leave unset for on-premise installs; the super-admin console
# (Phase 15) provisions businesses regardless of this setting.
# ALLOW_PUBLIC_SIGNUP=false

# Bidirectional server-to-server sync (Phase 11) — lets a café laptop (local
# server) and the VPS keep each other in sync so the café keeps working during
# an internet outage and catches up on reconnect.
#
# REMOTE_SYNC_TOKEN is the legacy shared bearer token.
# Since Phase 24, sync authenticates with per-business tokens and the shared
# fallback is denied by default. Only set this if legacy deployments haven't
# migrated yet, and you must set ALLOW_LEGACY_SYNC_TOKEN=1 to enable it.
# REMOTE_SYNC_TOKEN=
# ALLOW_LEGACY_SYNC_TOKEN=1

# Which side of the sync link this install is (Phase 23).
#
#   central — the VPS everyone syncs TO. Shows no "connect me to a central
#             server" form, and refuses writes to server_sync.config.
#   site    — a café laptop or desktop install that syncs to a central server.
#             Its central-server address is derived from pairing, not typed.
#
# When unset the role is inferred from the environment — central if
# REMOTE_SYNC_TOKEN, POS_DOMAIN or ROOT_DOMAIN is set, else site. Setting
# ROOT_DOMAIN means this deployment serves per-business subdomains, which only
# the central server does. The server logs which it chose, and where from, at
# startup.
# DEPLOYMENT_ROLE=site

# Base URL of the central server/platform, e.g. https://pos.eshobe.com. Used
# to derive the sync target on a site that has not been paired yet. Defaults
# to https://$POS_DOMAIN when that is set.
# PLATFORM_BASE_URL=

# Per-business subdomains (Phase 23).
#
# ROOT_DOMAIN is the zone every business origin hangs off, and setting it is
# what turns per-business origins on. Each business is served from
# {subdomain}.$ROOT_DOMAIN, the super-admin console from admin.$ROOT_DOMAIN,
# and the bare domain becomes a "which business?" router. Locally use
# `localtest.me`: every subdomain of it resolves to 127.0.0.1 over plain HTTP,
# so no hosts-file editing is needed.
#
# Setting ROOT_DOMAIN also makes this install read as the central server: the
# deployment role infers `central` whenever it is set, because only the
# platform serves per-business origins (see DEPLOYMENT_ROLE above).
#
# The root may itself be a subdomain. With ROOT_DOMAIN=ac.eshobe.com the
# businesses are biz1.ac.eshobe.com, biz2.ac.eshobe.com, … and the console is
# admin.ac.eshobe.com. What that costs is in DNS and TLS: the wildcard has to
# be *.ac.eshobe.com, since a certificate for *.eshobe.com does NOT cover a
# name one level deeper.
#
# Each business's subdomain is typed in English by a super-admin in
# /platform (never derived from the Persian business name), and it is the only
# thing that identifies a tenant in a URL — there is no /{slug}/ path prefix.
#
# A session cookie is valid only on its own business's host: that is the
# isolation this buys, and it is why the cookie deliberately carries no
# `domain` attribute (see sessionCookieOptions in auth-edge.ts).
#
# TRUST_FORWARDED_HOST is for deployments behind a managed platform (Runflare
# and most PaaS/CDN edges) rather than Traefik. Such a platform routes by
# hostname itself and hands the container an internal name — `Host:
# web-1234.internal:3000` — keeping the browser's hostname in
# `X-Forwarded-Host`. The app decides tenancy from `Host` by default, so there
# it would see an unknown host on every request, fail closed, and bounce
# authenticated visitors between the resolver and the apex until the platform's
# own health probe gives up and serves 502. Set it to `on` in that case, and
# leave it off behind Traefik (which passes the original Host through).
#
# The cost: a caller who can reach the app directly, bypassing the platform,
# can choose which origin their request appears to be on. Rows are scoped by
# the businessId inside the session JWT rather than by the host, so RLS is
# unaffected — but the origin check degrades to what the browser's cookie jar
# already enforces. Only turn it on when the platform is the only route in.
#
# `GET /api/host/resolve?debug=1` reports which header the app used and how it
# parsed, which is the fastest way to tell whether you need this.
#
# It also governs the `/ws` live-sync upgrade, whose Origin check compares
# against the host the *browser* used. Left off behind such a platform, every
# upgrade is rejected with 403 (the container's internal `Host` can never match
# a browser `Origin`), the socket reconnect-loops, and every dashboard shows
# "اتصال به سرور قطع است" while the app and Postgres are perfectly healthy —
# a symptom that looks like a database outage and is not one. Check
# `GET /api/health` (always 200 when the web tier is up) to tell the two apart.
# TRUST_FORWARDED_HOST=off
#
# SUBDOMAIN_ROUTING is only an escape hatch: set it to `off` to keep a
# deployment serving on one origin while its DNS or *wildcard* certificate is
# not ready (Let's Encrypt will not issue a wildcard over HTTP-01, so the
# Traefik certresolver must use a DNS-01 challenge). Leave it unset otherwise.
#
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID defaults to ROOT_DOMAIN, which is what biometric login needs
# here: a credential registered at one business's host stays valid at the
# deployment's other hosts, and an RP ID of a single business's host would make
# every ceremony elsewhere fail.
# ROOT_DOMAIN=localtest.me
# SUBDOMAIN_ROUTING=off

# Windows Print Connector distribution.
#
# The one-click installer in Printer Settings downloads the connector payload
# (a tenant-neutral file) from a *stable platform address*, never from each
# tenant's own hostname: a tenant subdomain whose DNS the in-browser page
# could reach (DoH) may still not resolve for the Windows system resolver
# that the installer uses. Resolution order:
#
#   1. CONNECTOR_DOWNLOAD_BASE_URL below, when set — an absolute http(s) URL,
#      optionally with a path prefix (a static-asset host or CDN works too);
#   2. the platform's own base (PLATFORM_BASE_URL, then POS_DOMAIN, then
#      ROOT_DOMAIN's apex) for requests that arrived over HTTPS;
#   3. the origin the request itself came in on — the right answer for
#      localhost, the desktop app and LAN/café-laptop installs, where the
#      same machine re-downloads from the same address.
#
# Leave unset for every standard deployment; set it only to pin a dedicated
# static host (e.g. https://downloads.example.com/cafe-pos). An invalid value
# is a configuration error: installer generation fails with a clear error
# rather than manufacturing a bad URL.
# CONNECTOR_DOWNLOAD_BASE_URL=
#
# Lowest connector protocol version the web app will accept before it asks
# for an update (default: the current protocol version).
# CONNECTOR_MIN_VERSION=3

# AI assistant (Phase 18 & Phase 39) — LiteLLM-only unified AI platform
# for every business and branch. Configure the technical connection in /platform/ai;
# customer pricing, allowance and wallet policy live in Plan/Billing.
# AI_ENABLED=false
# AI_PROVIDER=litellm
# AI_MODEL=pos-chat
# AI_BASE_URL=http://litellm:4000/v1
# AI_TEMPERATURE=0.3
# AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1000
# LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=
# APP_URL=https://your-pos.example.com

# LLM gateway — LiteLLM (Phase 37).
#
# Optional defaults for the LiteLLM gateway. `/platform/ai` manages only the
# technical connection and virtual-key lifecycle. LiteLLM's own config owns
# providers, routing, retries and fallback; Plan/Billing owns customer money.
#
# Configure connection parameters and model aliases in /platform/ai.
# If the gateway is enabled and virtual keys are on, each business gets its own
# LiteLLM virtual key (an identity: no mirrored budgets or rate limits), and
# the Rial credit balance in the platform wallet / plan allowance is the single billing stop.
#
# The model fields take the gateway's own aliases (the model_name values in
# docker/litellm/config.yaml). Routing, fallbacks, per-model limits and RPM/TPM live
# inside LiteLLM proxy configuration.
#
# LITELLM_ENABLED=false
# LITELLM_BASE_URL=http://litellm:4000/v1
# LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=
# LITELLM_CHAT_MODEL=pos-chat
# LITELLM_EMBEDDING_MODEL=pos-embed
# POS_MCP_URL=http://app:3000/api/mcp
# POS_MCP_TOKEN=

# Notifications / Web Push (Phase 35).
#
# Nothing here is required. The VAPID key pair is generated into
# platform_push_config on first use, because an on-site install has no operator
# to run a key-generation step — and a notification system that needed one would
# simply be off on every such install, silently.
#
# Set these only to make several deployments share one push identity. The public
# half is baked into every subscription a browser mints, so once devices are
# registered these must NOT be changed: every push would start failing with a
# 401 and nothing would say why.
#   VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY:  base64url, 65-byte uncompressed P-256 point
#   VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY: base64url, 32-byte scalar
#   VAPID_SUBJECT:     mailto: or https: URL a push service operator can use to
#                      reach whoever runs this deployment (RFC 8292 `sub`).
# Generate a pair with:
#   npx tsx -e "console.log(require('./src/lib/web-push').generateVapidKeys())"
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# Used by scripts/seed.ts for the initial Owner account
SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=owner@example.com
SEED_OWNER_PASSWORD=owner1234
SEED_OWNER_NAME=مالک نمونه

# Hardware printing — the app server renders receipts/kitchen tickets/labels
# to canonical ESC/POS bytes; delivery to real printers happens through the
# Cafe POS Windows Print Connector running on the cashier's own computer
# (loopback 127.0.0.1:9123). The connector owns BOTH hardware connection
# types: Windows printer queues (USB thermal printers included) and network
# ESC/POS printers (local LAN discovery + TCP delivery). Printer Settings
# provides an authenticated one-click connector install: no Node.js or manual
# configuration; it starts locally now and at every Windows login.
# PRINT_CHROMIUM_PATH / PDF_CHROMIUM_PATH: Chromium/Chrome/Edge executable
#   used by the SERVER to render receipts/tickets (correct Persian/RTL
#   shaping needs a real browser engine — see src/lib/escpos.ts). Optional:
#   when unset, the machine's own installed Chrome/Edge/Chromium is
#   auto-detected (src/lib/printing/chromium.ts).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRINT_CONNECTOR_URL: what the browser calls (must be loopback,
#   default http://127.0.0.1:9123 — normally never set).
# PRINT_CHROMIUM_PATH=/path/to/chromium
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRINT_CONNECTOR_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9123

# Backups (Phase 10) — schedule/retention/cloud settings live in the Owner
# dashboard (پشتیبان‌گیری); these env vars are machine-level paths/overrides.
# BACKUP_DIR: where pg_dump artifacts are written (default: ./backups).
# BACKUP_SECONDARY_DIR: optional second local target (mounted USB drive/NAS);
#   when set, every backup is also copied there and a failed copy fails the
#   run (so an unplugged drive surfaces on the dashboard).
# PG_DUMP_PATH / PG_RESTORE_PATH: binaries to use if not on PATH (the host
#   needs postgresql-client version >= the server's major version).
# BACKUP_DATABASE_URL: connection pg_dump uses. The app itself connects as the
#   restricted pos_app role (row-level security applies to it), and pg_dump
#   fails on such a role — "query would be affected by row-level security
#   policy for table ..." — so backups need the privileged/owner connection,
#   the same one migrations run with. Docker's entrypoint and the desktop
#   launcher set this automatically; set it by hand only if DATABASE_URL isn't
#   a privileged connection (e.g. managed Postgres where you provisioned
#   pos_app yourself).
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# BACKUP_SECONDARY_DIR=/mnt/usb-backup
# PG_DUMP_PATH=pg_dump
# PG_RESTORE_PATH=pg_restore
# BACKUP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://pos:pos@localhost:5432/pos

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Migration 0132 — the super-admin console's WHOLE-SYSTEM backup.
#
# Everything below is an optional *default*: the console (پشتیبان‌گیری زیر
# «سیستم») writes the same values into platform_backup_config, and a setting made
# there wins. The env exists for the two cases where the console cannot be
# reached — a brand-new server being migrated onto, and a machine whose database
# has just been restored over — plus deployments that want retention or the
# destination fixed by the operator rather than by whoever holds an admin login.
#
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_DIR: where whole-database artifacts are written. Default is
#   "$BACKUP_DIR/platform", a separate namespace on purpose: platform and
#   per-business artifacts share the pos-backup-<stamp>.dump name, so one flat
#   folder would let either side's retention prune the other's copies.
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_SECONDARY_DIR: second local target (NAS/USB). A failed copy
#   fails the run, so an unplugged drive is visible in the console.
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE: the AES-256-GCM key for platform artifacts. When
#   set, it is also the fallback for a stored-then-cleared console passphrase, and
#   a restore from another server cannot decrypt without it — keep it with the
#   machine, not in the database it protects.
# There is deliberately no env for the two peer switches (serving, and allowing
# plain http to a peer). They are console-only: a machine that can be switched
# into "anyone holding the key may download the entire company" by an unrelated
# deploy variable is not a machine you can audit, and the UI must never be able to
# show "off" while the server answers.
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES: the cap enforced *while streaming* a peer's
#   file to disk, so a peer that lies about content-length cannot fill this disk.
#   Default 2 GiB; values below 1 MiB are ignored as a typo.
# PG_DUMP_PATH / PG_RESTORE_PATH above are shared by both backup halves — the
#   platform restore needs the same client tools, and the same version rule
#   (pg_restore must be >= the pg_dump that wrote the file).
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_DIR=/mnt/nas/pos-platform
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_SECONDARY_DIR=/mnt/usb
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=
# PLATFORM_SERVE_BACKUPS=
# PLATFORM_ALLOW_INSECURE_PEERS=
# PLATFORM_BACKUP_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES=2147483648

# Restoring from cloud after total machine loss (scripts/restore.ts
# --from-cloud) — there is no local DB to read the dashboard's cloud settings
# from, so the S3 credentials and the encryption passphrase come from env:
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=
# BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Phase 24 Wave 3 — field-level encryption at rest.
#
# The key-encryption key (KEK) for the two-level key hierarchy. It never
# encrypts a row itself: it wraps each business's own data-encryption key in
# `business_encryption_keys`, and that DEK is what protects `customers.phone`,
# `customers.address`, `customers.notes` and `reservations.customer_phone`.
#
# Set ONE of these. With neither, field encryption is simply off and those
# columns stay plaintext exactly as they were before the wave — which is what
# keeps an existing install working across the release that adds the columns.
#
# POS_MASTER_KEY: 32 bytes, base64 or hex. Generate once, at install:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# POS_MASTER_PASSPHRASE: scrypt-derived instead, same parameters as the backup
#   passphrase. Convenient for a hand-run install; a real key is better.
#
# Keep it OUT of the database and out of any backup of it — a dump that carries
# both the wrapped DEKs and the KEK protects nothing. On the central VPS in a
# sync deployment, deliberately do NOT set it: the site box holds the key, the
# VPS holds ciphertext it cannot read, and central reporting therefore shows
# all money and ledger data (plaintext by design) but no customer phone or
# address.
#
# Losing it means losing every encrypted field. Back it up somewhere the
# database is not.
#
# After setting it on an existing install, backfill the ciphertext:
#   npm run db:encrypt-fields
# It is idempotent and resumable, so re-running is safe and cheap.
# POS_MASTER_KEY=
# POS_MASTER_PASSPHRASE=

# Phase 24 Wave 5 — durable (Postgres-backed) rate limiting.
#
# The limiter runs in middleware's Edge runtime, which cannot open a database
# connection, so it asks a Node-runtime route for the counter over HTTP. That
# call is authenticated with a secret derived from JWT_SECRET — nothing to set,
# and nothing new to rotate.
#
# By default it calls THIS process's own loopback listener
# (http://127.0.0.1:$PORT). That is deliberate: behind a proxy that terminates
# a public hostname the container often cannot route back to that hostname
# (no NAT hairpin, the proxy refusing its own public address, no local DNS),
# and a call aimed at the public origin then failed with "fetch failed" on
# every limited request — silently reinstating the per-process counter.
# Loopback never leaves the host, and the counter itself lives in Postgres,
# so durability and cross-replica sharing are unaffected.
#
# Set INTERNAL_BASE_URL only for a split-tier layout where the Node runtime
# serving /api/internal is a different host/container from the one running
# middleware.
#
# If the call cannot be made the limiter still works, but falls back to a
# per-process counter that resets on restart and is not shared across replicas
# — it logs loudly the first time this happens.
# INTERNAL_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenObserve monitoring (optional) — see docs/openobserve.md.
# The app ships a small log shipper (src/lib/observability.ts): console
# output, request errors and slow responses stream to an OpenObserve node,
# and the super-admin console's «سیستم → پایش» tab tails the same store.
# All three required vars unset → the feature is fully off (zero overhead,
# no new failure modes). Set them and restart, nothing else to enable.
# The central-server docker deployment gets all of this from
# archive/deploy/docker-compose.observability.yml; set them by hand only outside compose.
# Try the tab locally without Docker:  node scripts/dev-openobserve.mjs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPENOBSERVE_URL=http://localhost:5080
# OPENOBSERVE_USER=root@example.com
# OPENOBSERVE_PASSWORD=
# OPENOBSERVE_ORG=default
# OPENOBSERVE_LOG_STREAM=pos_app_logs
# OPENOBSERVE_SERVICE=pos-server
# OPENOBSERVE_ENV=development
# Where the operator's browser reaches the full OpenObserve UI:
# OPENOBSERVE_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:5080
# The website platform's own stream (migration 0139): every control call the
# console makes to eshobe-cms, plus the CMS's own event feed polled on a cursor.
# Deliberately separate from the app's stream — a retention window or an alert
# that suits this app's request log need not suit another product's audit tail.
# Read back by «سایت‌ساز → پایش»; see docs/openobserve.md.
# OPENOBSERVE_CMS_STREAM=cms_events

# Local/Hybrid Support + Bug Report relay. Provision a per-installation token
# through POST /api/platform/cloud-exceptions; it is shown once, stored hashed
# in Cloud, and never returned by a tenant API. A standalone installation gets
# an opaque support identity without becoming a Cloud operational tenant.
# SUPPORT_CLOUD_URL=https://pos.example.com
# SUPPORT_RELAY_TOKEN=
# SUPPORT_INSTALLATION_ID=
